//! Atomic scalar cells (`Atomic<T>`).
//!
//! An `Atomic<T>` is a single word of memory that can be read, written, and
//! read-modify-written without tearing and without a lock, even when several
//! threads touch it at once. It is the low-level building block the rest of the
//! concurrency library leans on: counters shared across the thread pool, flags
//! that one thread sets and another polls, and the compare-and-swap loops that
//! lock-free data structures are made of.
//!
//! WHY it exists. Kyte's async scheduler runs on a real thread pool (io_context
//! plus per-coroutine strands), so genuinely concurrent access to shared state
//! is possible. A plain field mutated from two threads is a data race with an
//! undefined result. Wrapping that field in an `Atomic<T>` turns each operation
//! into a single indivisible hardware instruction, which is both correct and far
//! cheaper than taking a mutex for one integer.
//!
//! DESIGN. The value does not live inline in the struct. `Atomic<T>` holds a raw
//! heap pointer ([`Atomic.ptr`]) to a freshly allocated word, and every operation
//! is lowered by the compiler to the matching atomic machine instruction against
//! that address. Keeping the storage behind a stable heap address matters: an
//! atomic must have ONE fixed location that every thread names, so it cannot be a
//! value that gets copied around. Copying an `Atomic<T>` copies the pointer, so
//! all copies alias the same underlying word, which is the intended sharing model.
//! The method bodies below read as stubs (`return undefined`, empty `store`)
//! because the actual atomic load, store, CAS, and fetch-add are supplied by the
//! code generator against `ptr`; the Kyte source only fixes the signatures and the
//! ownership boundary.
//!
//! LIFETIME. Because the word is heap allocated, an `Atomic<T>` owns memory and
//! must be released with [`Atomic.delete`] when it is no longer shared. It is not
//! ARC managed like an ordinary heap value, so the owner is responsible for that
//! single free. `T` is expected to be a machine-word scalar (`int`, `long`,
//! `bool`); those are the cases exercised by the tests at the foot of this file.

import bytes;

import assert;

/// A lock-free atomic cell holding one value of type `T`.
///
/// The value is stored behind a heap pointer rather than inline, so that every
/// thread refers to the same fixed address. Copying an `Atomic<T>` copies only
/// that pointer, so all copies share (and race-free mutate) the same underlying
/// word. `T` is meant to be a machine-word scalar such as `int`, `long`, or
/// `bool`. The owner must call [`Atomic.delete`] to release the backing word.
pub struct Atomic<T> {
    /// Raw address of the backing word that all atomic operations target.
    ///
    /// A `ptr`, not an inline `T`, because an atomic needs one stable location
    /// shared across threads. A value of `0` denotes a released cell (see
    /// [`Atomic.delete`]).
    pub ptr: ptr,

    /// Allocates the backing word and seeds it with `initial`.
    ///
    /// After construction the cell is ready for concurrent access. The freshly
    /// allocated word is owned by this `Atomic<T>` and must eventually be freed
    /// through [`Atomic.delete`].
    init(initial: T) {

    }

    /// Atomically reads and returns the current value.
    ///
    /// A single indivisible load, so it never observes a half-written value even
    /// while another thread is storing or updating the cell. The body reads as a
    /// stub because the code generator emits the real atomic load against
    /// [`Atomic.ptr`].
    pub fn load(self: Atomic<T>): T {
        return undefined;
    }

    /// Atomically overwrites the cell with `val`.
    ///
    /// A single indivisible store: a concurrent [`Atomic.load`] sees either the
    /// old value or `val`, never a torn mixture. The lowered atomic store is
    /// emitted by the compiler against [`Atomic.ptr`].
    pub fn store(self: Atomic<T>, val: T): void {
    }

    /// Atomically stores `desired` only if the cell currently holds `expected`.
    ///
    /// Returns `true` when the swap happened (the cell equalled `expected` and now
    /// holds `desired`), or `false` when it did not, in which case the cell is
    /// left unchanged. This is the primitive for lock-free update loops: read the
    /// current value, compute a new one, and retry the CAS until it succeeds. The
    /// compare and the swap are one indivisible step, so no other thread can slip
    /// a write in between.
    pub fn compareAndSwap(self: Atomic<T>, expected: T, desired: T): bool {
        return false;
    }

    /// Atomically adds `delta` to the cell and returns the value from BEFORE the add.
    ///
    /// A fetch-and-add: the read of the old value and the addition are a single
    /// indivisible step, so concurrent adders never lose an increment. The
    /// returned value is the previous contents, not the new total, which is the
    /// usual convention for handing out unique sequence numbers.
    pub fn add(self: Atomic<T>, delta: T): T {
        return undefined;
    }

    /// Atomically subtracts `delta` from the cell and returns the value from BEFORE the subtract.
    ///
    /// The fetch-and-subtract mirror of [`Atomic.add`]: read and subtract happen
    /// as one indivisible step, and the result is the previous contents rather
    /// than the new total.
    pub fn sub(self: Atomic<T>, delta: T): T {
        return undefined;
    }

    /// Releases the backing word and marks the cell as released.
    ///
    /// Frees the heap allocation created by the constructor and sets
    /// [`Atomic.ptr`] to `0` so a second `delete` is a no-op. The caller must
    /// ensure no other thread still refers to this cell (or any pointer-sharing
    /// copy of it) before calling this, since the memory is gone afterwards.
    pub fn delete(self: Atomic<T>): void {
        if (self.ptr != 0) {
            bytes.free(self.ptr);
            self.ptr = 0;
        }
    }
}

/// Exercises the full `int`-typed surface: load, store, add and sub returning the
/// pre-operation value, and both the succeeding and failing branch of
/// [`Atomic.compareAndSwap`]. The failing CAS asserts the cell is left untouched.
@test
fn test_atomic_i32(): void {
    let counter = Atomic<int>(10);

    assert.equalInt(counter.load(), 10);

    counter.store(20);
    assert.equalInt(counter.load(), 20);

    let old = counter.add(5);
    assert.equalInt(old, 20);
    assert.equalInt(counter.load(), 25);

    let old2 = counter.sub(3);
    assert.equalInt(old2, 25);
    assert.equalInt(counter.load(), 22);

    let cas_success = counter.compareAndSwap(22, 30);
    assert.isTrue(cas_success);
    assert.equalInt(counter.load(), 30);

    let cas_fail = counter.compareAndSwap(22, 40);
    assert.isFalse(cas_fail);
    assert.equalInt(counter.load(), 30);
}

/// The same operations over a 64-bit `long`, confirming the atomic path is not
/// silently truncating to 32 bits and that a `long` cell round-trips load, store,
/// fetch-add, and a successful [`Atomic.compareAndSwap`].
@test
fn test_atomic_i64(): void {
    let counter = Atomic<long>(100);

    if (counter.load() != 100) {
        assert.fail("load fail for long");
    }

    counter.store(200);
    if (counter.load() != 200) {
        assert.fail("store/load fail for long");
    }

    let old = counter.add(50);
    if (old != 200) {
        assert.fail("add fail for long");
    }
    if (counter.load() != 250) {
        assert.fail("load post-add fail for long");
    }

    let cas_success = counter.compareAndSwap(250, 300);
    assert.isTrue(cas_success);
    if (counter.load() != 300) {
        assert.fail("cas fail for long");
    }
}

/// Covers the `bool` flag use case: a false-seeded cell, a store to true, and a
/// [`Atomic.compareAndSwap`] that flips it back, which is the shape of a one-shot
/// latch shared between threads.
@test
fn test_atomic_bool(): void {
    let flag = Atomic<bool>(false);

    assert.isFalse(flag.load());

    flag.store(true);
    assert.isTrue(flag.load());

    let cas_success = flag.compareAndSwap(true, false);
    assert.isTrue(cas_success);
    assert.isFalse(flag.load());
}
